# file - tell what kind of file it is, from what is inside

## SYNOPSIS
    file [-b] file | pattern ...

## DESCRIPTION
Looks inside each file and says what it is, one line each:
`name: what`. It does not trust the name: a picture called `readme` is
still found to be a picture. Useful when you find a file without an
icon, or to check what a download really is.

What file knows:
- **AmigaOS** programs (with "pure" when the p bit is set; a library,
  device or resource when it says so), object files and link libraries,
  Workbench icons (.info: disk, drawer, tool, project ..., with OS 3.5
  colour images), IFF files (ILBM pictures with width, height and
  colours, HAM and EHB; 8SVX sounds with their rate; ANIM, SMUS, FTXT,
  AIFF, PREF and others by name), AmigaGuide, disk images (ADF by size
  and bootblock: OFS, FFS, international, cache; hard disk images with an
  RDB), PowerPacker and XPK data, DMS.
- **music** ProTracker-style MODs (with the channels and title),
  OctaMED, XM, Scream Tracker 3, Impulse Tracker, MIDI, WAV, MP3.
- **archives** LhA, LZX, Zip, gzip, bzip2, xz, 7-Zip, tar.
- **pictures** PNG, GIF, JPEG and BMP, with their size.
- **others** PDF, PostScript, ELF, MS-DOS and Windows programs.
- **text** ASCII, ISO-8859-1 (Latin-1, the Amiga's own) or UTF-8, saying
  when it has CRLF line ends or escape sequences. A text file with the s
  bit is an "AmigaDOS script"; one starting with `/\*` and with the s bit
  or a .rexx name is an "ARexx script".
- **the rest** drawer, volume, soft link (and where it points), empty,
  and "data" for anything not known.

## OPTIONS
- **-b** brief: only what it is, without `name: ` in front.

## EXAMPLES
    file Work:Downloads/#?
    file SYS:Libs/#?.library
    file -b DF0:mod.intro
    file S:Startup-Sequence S:User-Startup
    file Work:#? | grep -i ilbm

## RETURN CODES
0 fine, 5 a file could not be read or a pattern matched nothing,
10 bad arguments (no file named), 20 stopped with Ctrl-C.

## NOTES
Only the first 4 KB are read for most kinds, so file is quick even on
big files.

Patterns may be written `#?` or `\*`; upper and lower case are the same.

`file ?` shows the usage line.

